  "grammar": {
    "title": "Can and Can't",
    "explanation": "We use **can** for ability. I **can** swim, and I **can** say my address.\n\nWe use **can** for permission. **Can** I use your phone? Yes, you **can**.\n\nWe use **can** for requests. **Can** you pass the salt, please? **Can** you open the door for me?\n\nThe negative is **can't**. I **can't** drive. Penguins **can't** fly, but they **can** swim.\n\n**Can** is the same for every person. Use **can** plus the base verb, with no -s and no to.",
    "examples": [
      "*I **can** play the guitar.* (ability)",
      "*She **can't** come to the party.* (negative)",
      "* **Can** you speak Spanish? – Yes, I **can**.* (question + short answer)",
      "* **Can** I use your phone?* (permission)",
      "* **Can** you open the door for me, please?* (request)",
      "*Penguins **can't** fly, but they **can** swim very well.* (ability)"
    ],
    "practice": [
      {
        "q": "Rewrite with can: Sara is able to shop in Africa.",
